    <!-- Category Pill -->
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-xs font-semibold mb-4">
      <span>🇪🇸</span> Spanish Startup Act (Ley 28/2022) • International Tax Audit • Updated 2026
    </div>

    <!-- H1 Heading -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
      Spain Beckham Law vs Digital Nomad Visa: 24% Flat Tax vs Foreign Income Exemption Realities
    </h1>

    <!-- Quick Answer Box (Exact 45-60 words format with emerald accent border) -->
    <div class="border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-slate-900/80 border-t border-r border-b border-emerald-500/30 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10 shadow-2xl backdrop-blur-sm">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">
        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer: Spanish Digital Nomad Taxation Truth
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Holding a Spanish Digital Nomad Visa does <strong>not</strong> grant automatic foreign income exemption. Without the <strong>Beckham Law (Article 93 LIRPF)</strong> election, remote workers residing over 183 days face progressive tax rates reaching 47% on worldwide earnings. Beckham Law status fixes taxation at a flat <strong>24% up to €600,000</strong> while completely exempting non-Spanish investment dividends and foreign assets.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Executive Overview -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-200">
        One of the most dangerous and widespread misconceptions circulating in the international remote work community is that Spain’s Digital Nomad Visa (DNV)—created under the landmark <em>Ley de Fomento del Ecosistema de las Empresas Emergentes</em> (commonly known as the <strong>Ley de Startups</strong>, Law 28/2022)—functions as a territorial tax holiday. Internet marketing frequently conflates immigration authorization with fiscal non-residence.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Spanish jurisprudence, immigration permissions granted by the Unidad de Grandes Empresas y Colectivos Estratégicos (UGE-CE) are legally distinct from tax residence governed by the General Tax Law (Ley 58/2003, LGT) and the Personal Income Tax Law (Ley 35/2006, LIRPF). Simply obtaining a residency card (TIE) as a teleworker does not decouple your worldwide remuneration from the Spanish treasury (Agencia Estatal de Administración Tributaria, AEAT). Unless a founder or employee actively executes an opt-in filing under <strong>Article 93 of the LIRPF</strong>—the celebrated <strong>Special Impatriate Regime</strong>, colloquially known as the <strong>Beckham Law</strong>—they default into general tax residency, exposing every dollar, pound, and euro of global revenue to marginal rates exceeding 45% to 50% depending on the Autonomous Community.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Legal Foundation -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      1. Legal Architecture: Standard IRPF Worldwide Tax vs Article 93 Impatriate Regime
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        To understand the dichotomy between standard residency and the Beckham Law, one must evaluate the criteria governing tax residency in Spain under <strong>Article 9.1 of the LIRPF</strong>. An individual becomes an ordinary Spanish tax resident if they fulfill any of three statutory tests:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Physical Presence:</strong> Staying more than 183 days in Spanish territory during a natural calendar year (sporadic absences count as presence unless residency abroad for over 183 days is certified by a foreign tax authority).</li>
        <li><strong>Economic Nucleus:</strong> Having the primary base or direct/indirect core of your economic activities or financial interests located within Spain.</li>
        <li><strong>Familial Presumption:</strong> Having a legally non-separated spouse and dependent minor children who customarily reside in Spain (rebuttable presumption).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        When an ordinary tax residency triggers, the taxpayer is governed by the progressive scale of <em>Impuesto sobre la Renta de las Personas Físicas</em> (IRPF). Standard IRPF aggregates worldwide employment earnings, freelance invoices, corporate dividends, staking rewards, and capital gains into a global tax base.
      </p>
      <p>
        In contrast, the <strong>Beckham Law (Régimen Especial de Trabajadores Desplazados)</strong>, overhauled specifically to encompass international teleworkers under Law 28/2022, creates an artificial fiscal fiction: the taxpayer remains physically resident in Spain for immigration and living purposes, but is taxed under the rules of the <em>Impuesto sobre la Renta de No Residentes</em> (IRNR) for the year of arrival and the subsequent five tax years (totaling six fiscal periods).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Empirical Comparison Table 1: Comprehensive Tax Modeling -->
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Empirical Tax Liability Benchmark: Standard IRPF vs Beckham Law (2026)</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-1 shadow-2xl mb-8">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-slate-300">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-800">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3.5 font-bold text-white">Gross Annual Remuneration</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3.5 font-bold text-rose-400">Standard IRPF (Madrid)</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3.5 font-bold text-rose-400">Standard IRPF (Catalonia)</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3.5 font-bold text-emerald-400">Beckham Law (24% Flat)</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3.5 font-bold text-cyan-300">Net Beckham Advantage</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-xs font-mono">
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€45,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€10,240 (22.7%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€10,890 (24.2%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€10,800 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-amber-400 font-semibold">-€560 to +€90</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€65,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€18,480 (28.4%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€19,950 (30.7%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€15,600 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€2,880 to +€4,350</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€95,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€31,780 (33.4%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€34,600 (36.4%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€22,800 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€8,980 to +€11,800</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€150,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€58,180 (38.8%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€63,100 (42.1%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€36,000 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€22,180 to +€27,100</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€250,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€104,180 (41.7%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€113,100 (45.2%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€60,000 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€44,180 to +€53,100</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€600,000</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€261,680 (43.6%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">€288,100 (48.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€144,000 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€117,680 to +€144,100</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="p-3 text-[11px] text-slate-400 border-t border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/60 font-sans">
        *Note: Standard IRPF figures calculate combined state (tramo estatal) and regional (tramo autonómico) scales assuming single taxpayer without disability or dependent child deductions. Excludes employee Social Security deductions (max base capped at €4,720.50/mo in 2026).
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: The Break-Even Analysis -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      2. The Break-Even Income Threshold: When Does the Beckham Law Beat Progressive IRPF?
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Because the Beckham Law imposes an unyielding flat rate of <strong>24% starting from the very first euro earned</strong>, it does not provide the personal and family minimum allowances (<em>mínimo personal y familiar</em> of €5,550 base) or progressive lower tax brackets (19% on the first €12,450) available under standard IRPF.
      </p>
      <p>
        Consequently, an empirical break-even analysis reveals critical strategic nuances:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Below €45,000 Annual Gross:</strong> Electing the Beckham Law is actually financially detrimental. Under standard IRPF in communities like Madrid or Andalusia, personal allowances, deductible social security contributions, and progressive lower brackets produce an effective tax rate between 18.5% and 23.2%—lower than the 24% flat rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Between €50,000 and €60,000:</strong> The financial divergence is marginal (less than €1,500 difference). However, the administrative compliance cost of private gestor filing for Modelo 151 and initial Modelo 149 setup (€800–€1,500) may nullify tax arbitrage.</li>
        <li><strong>Above €65,000:</strong> The Beckham Law generates immediate and compounding alpha. At €100,000, the annual net cash retained exceeds €10,000. At €200,000, savings surpass €33,000 annually.</li>
        <li><strong>Above €600,000:</strong> Earnings above €600,000 are taxed at 47% under IRNR, matching top domestic brackets. However, the initial €600,000 chunk retains its locked 24% rate, permanently banking €138,000+ in annual tax differential.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Employee vs Autonomous Contractor (Autónomo) -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      3. Employee Remote Workers vs Freelance Contractors (Autónomos)
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Prior to the 2023 Startup Act reforms, independent contractors and self-employed individuals were categorical exclusions from the Beckham Law. The regime was historically restricted to senior multinational executives relocated by corporate parent entities. Law 28/2022 formally extended Article 93 eligibility to remote contractors holding the Digital Nomad Visa, but erected substantial structural conditions.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">The Three Qualifying Avenues for Digital Nomads</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-base mb-2 font-mono">Pathway A: Foreign Employment</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Direct employment contract with an enterprise outside Spain. Teleworking is authorized in writing by the company. Company cannot possess a permanent establishment (PE) in Spain.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-cyan-400 font-bold text-base mb-2 font-mono">Pathway B: Entrepreneur / Autonomo</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Providing services as an independent professional to foreign entities. A maximum of 20% of professional revenue may be invoiced to companies physically established in Spain.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-purple-400 font-bold text-base mb-2 font-mono">Pathway C: Startup Administrator</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Assuming a directorial role in an innovative enterprise certified by ENISA. Equity ownership caps were eliminated, allowing majority founder-CEOs to qualify.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>The Autónomo Trap:</strong> While independent contractors can now elect the Beckham Law, doing so creates a profound trade-off regarding professional expense deductions. Under standard IRPF, an autonomous contractor can deduct home office expenses, hardware depreciations, software licenses (AWS, GitHub, Datadog), accounting fees, and travel costs against their gross revenue before tax. Under Article 93, <em>no operational deductions whatsoever are permitted</em>; the 24% rate is levied on 100% of gross invoice volume.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Foreign Income Exemption Realities -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      4. Foreign Income Realities: Dividends, Capital Gains, and Crypto Assets
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The most transformative fiscal mechanism of the Beckham Law does not reside in the 24% earned income rate, but in its strict territorial ring-fencing of investment income (<em>rentas del capital</em>).
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-700 rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
        <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">§</span> Capital Taxation Comparison Matrix
        </h4>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-sm">
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-rose-900/40">
            <div class="font-bold text-rose-400 mb-2">Standard IRPF Tax Resident</div>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-300">
              <li>• Worldwide dividends taxed at progressive savings rates (19% to 28% in 2026).</li>
              <li>• Global stock sales and startup equity exits taxed at up to 28%.</li>
              <li>• Crypto token staking and capital gains taxed at 19%–28%.</li>
              <li>• Mandatory submission of Modelo 720 disclosing all offshore bank accounts, investment portfolios, and real estate exceeding €50,000.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-emerald-900/40">
            <div class="font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Beckham Law Impatriate Resident</div>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-300">
              <li>• <strong>0% Spanish Tax</strong> on foreign corporate dividends (US, UK, Estonia, etc.).</li>
              <li>• <strong>0% Spanish Tax</strong> on offshore capital gains (stock portfolio sales, foreign fund redemptions).</li>
              <li>• <strong>0% Spanish Tax</strong> on non-Spanish real estate rental profits.</li>
              <li>• <strong>100% Exemption from Modelo 720</strong> foreign asset reporting obligations.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <em>Caveat Emptor:</em> Only investment returns sourced outside of Spain qualify for this exemption. If an impatriate sells shares in a Spanish SL or receives rental dividends from an apartment in Valencia, those proceeds are classified as Spanish-source capital income and taxed under non-resident savings brackets (19%). Furthermore, carried interest from venture capital funds receives distinct statutory treatment depending on whether fund management takes place within Spanish borders.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Wealth Tax & Solidarity Tax -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      5. Wealth Tax (Patrimonio) and the Solidarity Tax (ITSGF) Shield
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Spain remains one of the few European jurisdictions maintaining an active net wealth tax (<em>Impuesto sobre el Patrimonio</em>), complemented by the nationwide Temporary Solidarity Tax on Large Fortunes (<em>Impuesto Temporal de Solidaridad de las Grandes Fortunas</em>, ITSGF), levying up to 3.5% annually on net wealth exceeding €3,000,000.
      </p>
      <p>
        For founders holding substantial liquid portfolios or high-valuation startup equity, ordinary Spanish tax residency can trigger crippling annual wealth tax assessments, even if no liquidity event has occurred.
      </p>
      <div class="border-l-4 border-cyan-500 bg-slate-900/70 p-5 rounded-r-xl">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-cyan-300 text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">The Real Estate Situs Limitation</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-300">
          Under Article 93, taxpayers are classified as non-residents for wealth taxation. By law, non-residents are subject to wealth tax exclusively under <em>obligación real</em> (real obligation)—meaning wealth tax applies <strong>only to assets physically situated or legally exercisable in Spain</strong>. A founder residing in Barcelona with an investment portfolio of €20,000,000 in Interactive Brokers US and Swiss custodian vaults pays exactly <strong>€0.00</strong> in Spanish Wealth and Solidarity Taxes under the Beckham Law.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Social Security & Totalization Agreements -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      6. Social Security Contributions: RETA vs Bilateral Totalization Certificates
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Taxation and Social Security are administered under completely independent regulatory codes in Spain. Qualifying for the Beckham Law does <strong>not</strong> grant an exemption from Social Security obligations (<em>Seguridad Social</em>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Spain operates two primary regimes for remote workers:
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <span class="text-white font-bold block mb-1">1. Régimen Especial de Trabajadores Autónomos (RETA)</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            For self-employed contractors. Under the progressive contribution system enacted in Royal Decree-Law 13/2022, monthly contributions are calculated on real net earnings (rendimientos netos). Monthly dues range from €230/month (base tier) up to €590/month (top tier for monthly net income over €6,000). First-time autónomos enjoy the <em>Tarifa Plana</em> of €80/month for the initial 12 months (extendable to 24 months if net income remains below statutory minimum wage).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <span class="text-white font-bold block mb-1">2. Régimen General (Employed Teleworkers)</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            Foreign employers must either register with the Spanish Social Security system as an employer (obtaining a Código de Cuenta de Cotización, CCC) and contribute roughly 31.4% in employer dues on top of the worker's 6.35% employee portion (capped at €4,720.50 monthly base), or establish an Employer of Record (EOR) structure.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <span class="text-white font-bold block mb-1">3. Bilateral Social Security Certificates of Coverage (Totalization)</span>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            If your employer is based in a country with a bilateral totalization treaty with Spain (e.g., the United States, United Kingdom, Canada), you may secure a certificate of coverage (such as an <strong>A1 Certificate</strong> within the EU/EEA, or <strong>US Form USA/E 101</strong>). This legally excuses both employer and employee from Spanish Social Security for up to 2 to 5 years, keeping contributions anchored in your home country.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Modelo 149 Deadlines & Critical Procedure -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      7. Procedural Roadmap: Filing Modelo 149, Strict 6-Month Deadlines & AEAT Scrutiny
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The Beckham Law election is an administrative tightrope. Missing the statutory filing window results in automatic and irrevocable forfeiture of the regime for the entirety of your stay in Spain.
      </p>
      <div class="relative pl-6 border-l-2 border-emerald-500 space-y-6 my-6">
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider">Step 1 • NIE and Social Security Affiliation</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1">
            Upon entering Spain under the DNV or getting your consular resolution approved, obtain your NIE and register with Social Security or verify your home-country Certificate of Coverage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider">Step 2 • The Inviolable 6-Month Window</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1">
            Under Article 116 of the Personal Income Tax Regulations (RD 439/2007), <strong>Modelo 149</strong> must be formally filed electronically with the AEAT within <strong>exactly 6 months</strong> from the date of registration in the Spanish Social Security system, or the date of commencement of employment activity specified on the documentation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider">Step 3 • AEAT Resolution & Resolving Certificate</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1">
            AEAT has a statutory 10-business-day window to acknowledge receipt and typically issues the formal resolution certificate (<em>Certificado de concesión del régimen especial</em>) within 1 to 3 months. Once approved, present this certificate to your employer or payroll administrator to withhold at the flat 24% rate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider">Step 4 • Annual Filing via Modelo 151</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 mt-1">
            Impatriates do not submit the standard resident Modelo 100 tax return between April and June. Instead, they file <strong>Modelo 151</strong>, reporting exclusively Spanish-sourced earned income, exempting foreign assets and foreign bank accounts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">Automated Modelo 149 Eligibility & Deadline Verification</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mb-4">
        Below is a reference Python validation script modeling the strict 183-day lookback rule, non-residence 5-year history check (lowered from 10 years by Law 28/2022), and deadline calculation:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 p-4">
        <pre is:raw class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>from datetime import datetime, timedelta

def verify_beckham_law_eligibility(
    prior_spanish_tax_residence_years: list[int],
    social_security_registration_date: str,
    annual_gross_income_eur: float,
    foreign_capital_gains_eur: float
) -> dict:
    current_year = datetime.now().year
    # Statutory Rule 1: No Spanish tax residence in prior 5 natural tax years
    prohibited_years = [current_year - i for i in range(1, 6)]
    violates_residency = any(y in prohibited_years for y in prior_spanish_tax_residence_years)
    
    # Statutory Rule 2: Strict 6-month filing window from Social Security date
    reg_date = datetime.strptime(social_security_registration_date, "%Y-%m-%d")
    filing_deadline = reg_date + timedelta(days=180) # AEAT strictly audits 6 calendar months
    days_remaining = (filing_deadline - datetime.now()).days

    # Mathematical savings simulation
    # Madrid standard progressive rate approximation vs 24% IRNR flat
    def calc_standard_irpf_madrid(gross: float) -> float:
        brackets = [
            (12450, 0.185), (20200, 0.235), (35200, 0.285),
            (60000, 0.365), (300000, 0.445), (float('inf'), 0.450)
        ]
        tax, prev = 0.0, 0.0
        for limit, rate in brackets:
            if gross > limit:
                tax += (limit - prev) * rate
                prev = limit
            else:
                tax += (gross - prev) * rate
                break
        return tax

    std_tax = calc_standard_irpf_madrid(annual_gross_income_eur)
    # Plus capital gains tax on foreign gains under standard IRPF (up to 28%)
    std_cap_tax = foreign_capital_gains_eur * 0.27
    
    # Beckham Law calculation
    beckham_earned_tax = min(annual_gross_income_eur, 600000) * 0.24
    if annual_gross_income_eur > 600000:
        beckham_earned_tax += (annual_gross_income_eur - 600000) * 0.47
    beckham_cap_tax = 0.0 # 100% exempt on foreign investment gains

    total_std = std_tax + std_cap_tax
    total_beckham = beckham_earned_tax + beckham_cap_tax
    annual_delta = total_std - total_beckham

    return {
        "eligible_5yr_non_residence": not violates_residency,
        "filing_deadline": filing_deadline.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"),
        "days_remaining_to_file": max(0, days_remaining),
        "standard_total_tax_eur": round(total_std, 2),
        "beckham_total_tax_eur": round(total_beckham, 2),
        "annual_net_benefit_eur": round(annual_delta, 2),
        "recommendation": "ELECT BECKHAM LAW" if annual_delta > 0 and not violates_residency else "STANDARD IRPF PREFERABLE"
    }

# Benchmark execution: €120,000 remote salary with €30,000 US stock dividends
result = verify_beckham_law_eligibility(
    prior_spanish_tax_residence_years=[],
    social_security_registration_date="2026-04-15",
    annual_gross_income_eur=120000,
    foreign_capital_gains_eur=30000
)
print(result)
</code></pre>
